{
  "id": "fan-out-convergence",
  "title": "Fan-Out Convergence",
  "category": "ad-chain",
  "source": ["Relia"],
  "designed_by": "lab-derived",
  "description": "Largest lab: 16 machines across 2 networks. Multiple external entry points (RiteCMS, Apache CVE, Umbraco, DNN SMB, phishing) converge via .249 git creds → .189 MAIL phishing → .14 WK01. Internal sweep via .21 PS transcript → domain admin → psexec. Linux: .245/.246 SSH chain, .19 borg/pspy, .20 BSD doas.",
  "introduction": "Teaches multi-vector convergence: many entry points lead to one internal bridge. OSCP-relevant for managing complexity—track which creds unlock which path; phishing or SMB may be the only way in.",
  "mental_model": "Map all external footholds first; credentials from one (git, KeePass, FTP) may bridge to internal. SMB shares and PS transcripts often contain domain admin passwords. Follow the credential, not the machine.",
  "key_principles": [
    "Multiple entry points converge—git creds enable phishing; KeePass holds many",
    "SMB enum with low-priv creds; scripts/monitoring shares have hardcoded passwords",
    "PowerShell transcripts and history files leak domain admin creds",
    "Phishing (Library-ms + WebDAV) may be only external→internal bridge",
    "pspy reveals cron passphrases; doas/sudo misconfigs for Linux privesc"
  ],

  "indicators": {
    "difficulty": 8,
    "cognitive_load": 9,
    "enumeration_depth": 7,
    "rabbit_hole_risk": 7,
    "time_pressure": 10
  },

